1
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:30,732
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a question from a friend.

2
00:00:31,953 --> 00:00:42,482
[SPEAKER_00]: He wants to know, Bhante, how could Arahant Angulimala transform from a mass murderer to an Arahant?

3
00:00:42,722 --> 00:00:43,823
[SPEAKER_00]: How could Angulimala?

4
00:00:45,324 --> 00:00:50,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Can someone grasp the dhamma deeply despite such a dark past?

5
00:00:51,188 --> 00:00:54,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Was there no guilt or shame for his deeds?

6
00:00:55,372 --> 00:00:56,773
[SPEAKER_01]: So, well,

7
00:00:58,293 --> 00:01:08,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, technically, somebody can grasp the dhamma, despite such, like, severe actions, like what Angulimala did.

8
00:01:09,383 --> 00:01:11,606
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, those, you know, would be far and few.

9
00:01:11,666 --> 00:01:18,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Even back in the Buddhist time, when it was the most optimal time for the practice of the dhamma, he was, like, one in a million.

10
00:01:20,374 --> 00:01:21,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Far more people

11
00:01:22,249 --> 00:01:28,872
[SPEAKER_01]: There are many more murderers and people that committed crimes that, because of that, it would prevent them from understanding the dhamma.

12
00:01:29,312 --> 00:01:34,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the king, who killed his father, the previous king.

13
00:01:34,434 --> 00:01:43,397
[SPEAKER_01]: When he went to listen to the Buddha, and the Buddha kind of taught him the dhamma and everything, and he was rejoicing, and he was, like, very inspired, but he did not become a sotāpanna.

14
00:01:44,058 --> 00:01:47,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And when he left, the Buddha said, oh, it's because he killed his father,

15
00:01:48,418 --> 00:01:52,782
[SPEAKER_01]: If he didn't kill his father, he would have got a right view, right here, right now, upon listening to me.

16
00:01:52,822 --> 00:01:53,963
[SPEAKER_01]: But he didn't, because of that.

17
00:01:54,003 --> 00:01:54,183
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

18
00:01:54,664 --> 00:01:59,748
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you might say, oh, there's some magical connection between, oh, he committed murder, thus it prevents him from the dhamma.

19
00:01:59,768 --> 00:02:01,510
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's actually...

20
00:02:01,530 --> 00:02:10,337
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason that prevented him from understanding the dhamma, there and then, when the Buddha was teaching him face to face, was also present there and then.

21
00:02:10,357 --> 00:02:13,060
[SPEAKER_01]: That reason, because of which he did not become a Sotāpanna.

22
00:02:14,968 --> 00:02:18,571
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason being, yes, he killed the father, but see, Angulimala killed many people.

23
00:02:19,672 --> 00:02:21,333
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, he was able to turn it around.

24
00:02:21,473 --> 00:02:23,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's the difference between the two?

25
00:02:23,675 --> 00:02:42,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the difference is, contrary to lack of shame and lack of remorse for what he did, Angulimala was actually able to take the weight of all of his actions, transparently, authentically, and feel the full extent of what he did.

26
00:02:43,691 --> 00:02:47,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, most people would probably go mad, if they were to accept it.

27
00:02:48,213 --> 00:03:01,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, everybody, you know, who... who basically... who commits such heinous acts, has to have some sort of excuse, some sort of cover-up, some sort of... kind of... blurring of responsibility.

28
00:03:01,719 --> 00:03:03,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Has to... cannot take it upon himself.

29
00:03:04,481 --> 00:03:04,661
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

30
00:03:04,921 --> 00:03:09,603
[SPEAKER_01]: I did this, and fully experienced the... the entire extent of that action.

31
00:03:10,023 --> 00:03:11,304
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why that king,

32
00:03:12,125 --> 00:03:14,386
[SPEAKER_01]: who killed his father did not become a Sotāpanna.

33
00:03:14,786 --> 00:03:17,228
[SPEAKER_01]: He could not admit to himself.

34
00:03:17,308 --> 00:03:22,070
[SPEAKER_01]: He could not accept fully what he did.

35
00:03:22,090 --> 00:03:24,111
[SPEAKER_01]: The weight, because it would have crushed him.

36
00:03:24,171 --> 00:03:25,592
[SPEAKER_01]: The remorse would have crushed him.

37
00:03:25,612 --> 00:03:28,053
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, his own father who raised him and everything.

38
00:03:28,714 --> 00:03:34,497
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm sure he'd instead of... he came probably close to accepting it upon listening to the Buddha, but chose to

39
00:03:35,197 --> 00:03:43,183
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, double down towards his reasoning, towards his justifications, for whatever reasons he would have done such a thing.

40
00:03:44,504 --> 00:03:45,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Angulimala didn't do that.

41
00:03:46,986 --> 00:03:52,731
[SPEAKER_01]: When he met the Buddha, when the Buddha gave him the short teaching, he sort of cut through all of it, and he was actually able to accept it.

42
00:03:52,811 --> 00:03:54,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why he was able to become an arhant.

43
00:03:54,332 --> 00:04:04,480
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that, like, very drastic, non-compromising authenticity of taking responsibility for what you did,

44
00:04:05,190 --> 00:04:10,613
[SPEAKER_01]: What you've been doing, how you've been acting, and infinitely taking that responsibility.

45
00:04:10,653 --> 00:04:13,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, until tomorrow and then tomorrow I'll become an arahant or something.

46
00:04:14,154 --> 00:04:14,455
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

47
00:04:14,495 --> 00:04:15,815
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know how long it will take.

48
00:04:15,855 --> 00:04:16,976
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even know you're gonna succeed.

49
00:04:17,376 --> 00:04:18,116
[SPEAKER_01]: That's secondary.

50
00:04:18,496 --> 00:04:21,358
[SPEAKER_01]: First and foremost is, you gotta accept what you did.

51
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:29,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And, basically, learn to live with it, so to speak, without covering up, without always because of this, or I was in this state, or that's it.

52
00:04:29,896 --> 00:04:30,096
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

53
00:04:30,696 --> 00:04:36,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever that might have been, those extended circumstances, still, I did those things because I consciously chose to do them.

54
00:04:38,242 --> 00:04:40,164
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is a prerequisite for understanding the dhamma.

55
00:04:40,584 --> 00:04:50,432
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the difference why somebody like Angulimala, who committed so many murders, and who knows what else, was actually able to become an arahant,

56
00:04:51,481 --> 00:04:58,445
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, some people who didn't even commit a murder, but were kind of immoral in some smaller ways, that prevented them from becoming even a Sotāpanna.

57
00:04:59,726 --> 00:05:01,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they were not admitting it to themselves.

58
00:05:01,827 --> 00:05:05,329
[SPEAKER_01]: They were not really allowing the weight of their action to sink in.

59
00:05:06,629 --> 00:05:10,552
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see that in mundane examples, day-to-day life.

60
00:05:11,172 --> 00:05:20,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Generally, people avoid anything that would produce a sense of shame in them, guilt or remorse.

61
00:05:21,624 --> 00:05:30,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, they don't necessarily avoid it beforehand, before they do it, but they tend to avoid taking responsibility after they've done such things, through carelessness.

62
00:05:31,291 --> 00:05:44,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you act carelessly, you break the precepts, you misbehave and so on, and then afterwards, you sort of have... when you stop, you have an option to now accept and feel bad for what you did and endure it for as long as it takes, until the mind kind of grows out of it.

63
00:05:45,655 --> 00:05:54,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you kind of turn a blind eye, and then double down, and continue doing more, to just keep you... to keep yourself in that perpetual state of motion, so you don't have to stop and think about it, what you did.

64
00:05:57,027 --> 00:06:04,871
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... if that attitude is not abandoned, if that's not changed, there is absolutely no any... no basis for any practice of the dhamma whatsoever.

65
00:06:05,292 --> 00:06:18,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how many suttas you read, how strictly you have to first admit, accept the weight of unwholesome and, if possible, even, like, confess it.

66
00:06:18,799 --> 00:06:23,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Confess your shortcoming, your transgressions, like the suttas would say.

67
00:06:25,972 --> 00:06:27,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any cover-ups?

68
00:06:27,573 --> 00:06:28,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Cover-ups, basically.

69
00:06:28,893 --> 00:06:29,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Internal cover-ups.

70
00:06:30,013 --> 00:06:31,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you have to bring them out to light.

71
00:06:32,094 --> 00:06:42,818
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to let me, like, to go publicly now and declaring to everyone what you did, but you have to do it yourself, and you also have to do it in a sense that if it were to be brought up, you will not cover it up.

72
00:06:42,978 --> 00:06:44,038
[SPEAKER_01]: You will say how it is.

73
00:06:45,679 --> 00:06:55,002
[SPEAKER_01]: There was that another example of a monk on his deathbed being ill, and kind of... and other monks asked the Buddha to go and see him, because apparently he wasn't... he didn't even have the right view.

74
00:06:56,198 --> 00:07:15,262
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's about to die, so the Buddha goes and asks him, and he says, yeah, like, you know, he still didn't understand the teaching, he's not a sotāpanna, and that's... and basically he was like, you know, he was sort of remorseful, and a bit, I don't know, mental anguish, and the Buddha says, why are you remorseful?

75
00:07:16,081 --> 00:07:17,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you didn't... you did not become a sotāpanna.

76
00:07:17,742 --> 00:07:21,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because, you know, you did things by body, speech and mind that you have not confessed?

77
00:07:21,483 --> 00:07:22,663
[SPEAKER_01]: That you have not brought to light?

78
00:07:23,144 --> 00:07:24,324
[SPEAKER_01]: That you have not made transparent?

79
00:07:24,344 --> 00:07:25,004
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, no, no, no, no.

80
00:07:25,264 --> 00:07:25,384
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

81
00:07:26,065 --> 00:07:26,405
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been...

82
00:07:26,445 --> 00:07:26,865
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been pure.

83
00:07:26,905 --> 00:07:27,745
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been behaving well.

84
00:07:28,025 --> 00:07:29,786
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that I still haven't understood the dhamma.

85
00:07:29,806 --> 00:07:31,607
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the sole reason for my remorse.

86
00:07:31,647 --> 00:07:34,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha taught him the dhamma and he became a sotāpanna before he died.

87
00:07:35,409 --> 00:07:36,070
[SPEAKER_01]: He understood it.

88
00:07:36,911 --> 00:07:38,453
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is a factor.

89
00:07:38,493 --> 00:07:47,804
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a factor, that kind of drastic acceptance of responsibility, as we often talk about.

90
00:07:47,865 --> 00:07:52,991
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, we had many talks about the role of responsibility and freedom from suffering and so on.

91
00:07:57,704 --> 00:07:59,406
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a second part of this question.

92
00:07:59,466 --> 00:08:06,691
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, I wonder why, despite not committing such actions and earnestly seeking dhamma, I still struggle to reach arahantship.

93
00:08:06,791 --> 00:08:07,131
[SPEAKER_01]: There you go.

94
00:08:07,611 --> 00:08:08,132
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the answer.

95
00:08:10,073 --> 00:08:16,177
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to, you have to, and like, if you're like, well, I haven't done anything bad, it's relative, you know.

96
00:08:16,517 --> 00:08:21,421
[SPEAKER_01]: For a small mind, even minor little kind of infractions and actions

97
00:08:22,538 --> 00:08:24,240
[SPEAKER_01]: can't be more than enough to pollute it.

98
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:31,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the Buddha gave that example, you know, you put a, you put a, like, I don't know, a drop of dye into a glass.

99
00:08:32,389 --> 00:08:34,211
[SPEAKER_01]: It's gonna dye the whole amount of water.

100
00:08:34,952 --> 00:08:37,374
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you put a drop of dye in an ocean.

101
00:08:38,095 --> 00:08:39,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not gonna make a difference.

102
00:08:40,198 --> 00:08:41,239
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't know

103
00:08:42,430 --> 00:08:43,571
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how developed your mind is.

104
00:08:43,712 --> 00:08:52,682
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is, if you're not getting the right view, if you're not progressing in the dhamma, keeping the precepts means, okay, so you need to dig deeper, basically.

105
00:08:54,184 --> 00:09:01,133
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be more serious with your sense restraint, more serious with avoiding distractions.

106
00:09:02,214 --> 00:09:12,759
[SPEAKER_01]: We're serious with kind of giving in to these little ways out of enduring the pressure of withdrawal, of saying no to sensuality.

107
00:09:13,599 --> 00:09:14,620
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, see what comes up.

108
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:19,202
[SPEAKER_01]: What's going to kind of... That pressure will bring something to the surface.

109
00:09:21,403 --> 00:09:23,604
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, see what mind starts revolving around.

110
00:09:23,684 --> 00:09:24,645
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you remorseful?

111
00:09:25,185 --> 00:09:29,227
[SPEAKER_01]: And not in a sense, I have to go and deal with this before I can start practicing.

112
00:09:29,247 --> 00:09:29,487
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

113
00:09:29,867 --> 00:09:31,528
[SPEAKER_01]: You're dealing with this by bringing it up.

114
00:09:32,350 --> 00:09:34,112
[SPEAKER_01]: You're dealing with it by not covering it up.

115
00:09:36,274 --> 00:09:41,499
[SPEAKER_01]: And quite contrary to the popular expectation, you're not actually trying to resolve it.

116
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:47,664
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to train your mind, so it stops resisting it, while it's there.

117
00:09:48,105 --> 00:09:50,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't want to resolve it, in that sense.

118
00:09:50,667 --> 00:09:51,868
[SPEAKER_01]: When you bring these things up,

119
00:09:53,345 --> 00:10:01,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, as I said, you know, if it's like major breaches of precepts, that needs to be confessed, that needs to be referred to somebody else as well.

120
00:10:01,291 --> 00:10:03,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Just... just you make it public in that sense.

121
00:10:03,232 --> 00:10:04,773
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes a difference to your state of mind.

122
00:10:05,553 --> 00:10:13,779
[SPEAKER_01]: But now we're talking about, you know, somebody didn't do anything bad necessarily, hasn't harmed anybody, hasn't... didn't steal anything, nothing like that, but still not progressing in the dhamma.

123
00:10:15,708 --> 00:10:23,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to start digging in further within yourself, in that sense of sense restraint, that will bring up various, you know...

124
00:10:24,935 --> 00:10:26,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, neurotic tendencies, to put it that way.

125
00:10:27,156 --> 00:10:28,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind might resist that.

126
00:10:28,837 --> 00:10:34,680
[SPEAKER_01]: It will start to push you, to make you act out towards sensuality or ill-will and so on.

127
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:38,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you're going to start feeling guilty on account of things that are kind of irrational.

128
00:10:39,003 --> 00:10:40,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to go through that.

129
00:10:40,063 --> 00:10:45,446
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically like a mind that has been caught or that has been sort of trapped.

130
00:10:46,987 --> 00:10:51,870
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to start to kick and scream and resist initially and throw everything it has at you.

131
00:10:54,037 --> 00:10:54,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Until it calms down.

132
00:10:55,498 --> 00:10:56,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the point.

133
00:10:56,919 --> 00:11:04,385
[SPEAKER_01]: You're bringing all of that up, so to teach your mind how to calm down within these things that are assailing you.

134
00:11:04,425 --> 00:11:08,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to calm down by resolving them and making them go away.

135
00:11:08,788 --> 00:11:08,908
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

136
00:11:09,128 --> 00:11:12,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were calm while they're there, that's how they go away.

137
00:11:13,792 --> 00:11:15,994
[SPEAKER_01]: If mind is calmed down within those things,

138
00:11:16,843 --> 00:11:29,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that pressure of responsibility, within the neurotic kind of tendencies, within everything else, like your thoughts wanting to make you act towards sensuality, your mind does not act, endures it, calms down.

139
00:11:30,251 --> 00:11:38,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, the Buddha described it so many times, like an elephant in the battle, that's being prodded by spears, with whips, whatever, and it doesn't flinch.

140
00:11:39,797 --> 00:11:41,098
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you need to train your mind.

141
00:11:41,118 --> 00:11:42,619
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what it is.

142
00:11:42,679 --> 00:11:43,960
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of samādhi.

143
00:11:45,125 --> 00:11:54,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Angulimala obviously did that, despite the weight of his actions, the weight of his remorse, the weight of his regret, the consequences of everything he did.

144
00:11:54,595 --> 00:11:56,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything was reminding him of what he did.

145
00:11:56,978 --> 00:12:00,802
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, people were beating him up on the street, throwing stones at him, even later years.

146
00:12:02,584 --> 00:12:04,666
[SPEAKER_01]: But he endured it all, and the Buddha actually told him to bear it.

147
00:12:07,241 --> 00:12:09,603
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically... that's the extent of the punishment.

148
00:12:09,623 --> 00:12:09,943
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

149
00:12:10,223 --> 00:12:20,311
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not going to have more, because you have freed your mind to fully accept the responsibility for that, that then enable you to understand the dhamma and the actual root of suffering and the way out of suffering.

150
00:12:22,633 --> 00:12:34,203
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is to train your mind within the container of these unpleasant emotions, weight of responsibility and so on.

151
00:12:35,193 --> 00:12:42,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Train it in a sense of learn how to keep it calm without trying to get rid of what's sort of bothering you.

152
00:12:43,358 --> 00:12:53,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you know, so Angulimala, whatever happened in his life, whatever pressure arose for him, made him so angry, so violent.

153
00:12:54,084 --> 00:12:54,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

154
00:12:54,324 --> 00:12:55,125
[SPEAKER_00]: He was killing people.

155
00:12:55,225 --> 00:12:56,525
[SPEAKER_00]: He was doing a lot of stuff, yeah.

156
00:12:56,545 --> 00:12:57,846
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, the only way to

157
00:12:59,287 --> 00:13:06,485
[SPEAKER_00]: To uproot that, those tendencies of violence and anger and so on, you would have to face that pressure which he...

158
00:13:06,871 --> 00:13:10,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Which he's been running away with his whole life, that made him do such bad actions.

159
00:13:10,813 --> 00:13:18,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, not just that he had to accept that pressure, he now had to accept the whole amount of the bad actions that he did on account of that pressure.

160
00:13:18,396 --> 00:13:18,877
[SPEAKER_01]: And he did.

161
00:13:19,817 --> 00:13:25,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And so that the tendency to lash out violently, he did.

162
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:27,241
[SPEAKER_00]: He has to bear that.

163
00:13:27,561 --> 00:13:29,262
[SPEAKER_00]: He has to bear that, yeah.

164
00:13:30,682 --> 00:13:34,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, a serious habit, like a habit that had serious consequences, exactly.

165
00:13:35,583 --> 00:13:36,603
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that's my point.

166
00:13:36,623 --> 00:13:45,405
[SPEAKER_01]: See, it doesn't matter whether it was something as severe as Angulimala's case, or some minor things you're doing, but the principle remains the same.

167
00:13:45,585 --> 00:13:46,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you acting out?

168
00:13:47,125 --> 00:13:48,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you distracting yourself?

169
00:13:48,326 --> 00:13:49,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you covering up?

170
00:13:49,126 --> 00:13:51,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you not enduring things on the right level?

171
00:13:51,526 --> 00:13:58,308
[SPEAKER_01]: And by that, I mean, see, you have an unpleasant emotion present itself on account of something you did, or something you said, or somebody else did, or somebody else said.

172
00:13:58,328 --> 00:14:00,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you trying to get rid of it?

173
00:14:01,152 --> 00:14:03,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you trying to psychologize it, explain it?

174
00:14:04,355 --> 00:14:04,535
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

175
00:14:04,995 --> 00:14:06,617
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already acting out.

176
00:14:07,757 --> 00:14:19,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Learn how to remain unmoved by it, and when you succeed, well, then you won't care whether it's coming or going, that thing that's pressuring you, whether it's present or it's not present, because either way you're not moving.

177
00:14:21,540 --> 00:14:23,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Without needing to do anything on account of it.

178
00:14:23,442 --> 00:14:24,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Without needing to act out.

179
00:14:25,064 --> 00:14:27,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Without needing to meditate and concentrate away.

180
00:14:27,667 --> 00:14:29,049
[SPEAKER_01]: And none of that's necessary then.

181
00:14:29,129 --> 00:14:32,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all of that was done because you were moved in the first place.

182
00:14:35,831 --> 00:14:39,935
[SPEAKER_00]: He learned how to no longer crave or resist against pain.

183
00:14:40,175 --> 00:14:48,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much, he freed himself from that fundamental patīgā, fundamental resistance towards his own form, towards his own state of being.

184
00:14:48,301 --> 00:14:48,982
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's...

185
00:14:49,122 --> 00:14:51,784
[SPEAKER_00]: I think his name was then... he was then called Ahiṁsā.

186
00:14:51,804 --> 00:14:52,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Ahiṁsā, yeah.

187
00:14:52,565 --> 00:14:53,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-violent.

188
00:14:53,285 --> 00:14:54,066
[SPEAKER_01]: The non-violent one.

189
00:14:54,086 --> 00:14:57,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he was... well, again, he had to so drastically develop

190
00:14:58,549 --> 00:15:04,474
[SPEAKER_01]: The attitude of non-violence, because of his violent attitude before, that that became his defining characteristic.

191
00:15:05,335 --> 00:15:06,616
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the thing.

192
00:15:06,636 --> 00:15:18,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even though I'm not a killer, I don't murder people and so on, but I get annoyed at mosquitoes, there's that root of murder.

193
00:15:19,006 --> 00:15:19,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Resistance, yeah.

194
00:15:21,708 --> 00:15:26,051
[SPEAKER_01]: I get annoyed at mosquitoes or at sounds.

195
00:15:26,431 --> 00:15:28,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Sounds are bothering me.

196
00:15:28,313 --> 00:15:33,857
[SPEAKER_01]: What somebody's, like, saying or what I'm hearing is just bothering me as a neutral sound.

197
00:15:34,978 --> 00:15:36,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Boredom as well.

198
00:15:36,459 --> 00:15:38,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, like, I fear it.

199
00:15:38,200 --> 00:15:39,061
[SPEAKER_01]: I need something to do.

200
00:15:39,101 --> 00:15:40,462
[SPEAKER_01]: I need something to keep me occupied.

201
00:15:40,482 --> 00:15:42,503
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't accept my state of being for what it is.

202
00:15:42,863 --> 00:15:43,524
[SPEAKER_01]: I resist it.

203
00:15:45,378 --> 00:15:45,998
[SPEAKER_01]: I resist it.

204
00:15:46,218 --> 00:15:47,038
[SPEAKER_01]: I become cranky.

205
00:15:47,178 --> 00:15:48,599
[SPEAKER_01]: When I'm cranky, I lash out.

206
00:15:49,259 --> 00:15:56,680
[SPEAKER_01]: When I lash out, I basically, publicly, sort of, perform actions that are unsuitable.

207
00:15:57,360 --> 00:15:58,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I'm ashamed.

208
00:15:58,020 --> 00:15:59,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I cover up the shame.

209
00:15:59,981 --> 00:16:01,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I justify it.

210
00:16:01,141 --> 00:16:02,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I say it's because of this or that.

211
00:16:02,561 --> 00:16:06,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I continue lash... but continue perpetuating the same principles.

212
00:16:06,062 --> 00:16:07,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the next time it happens, same.

213
00:16:07,582 --> 00:16:10,422
[SPEAKER_01]: You go through the same motion, same cycle.

214
00:16:10,442 --> 00:16:11,243
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to accept it.

215
00:16:11,323 --> 00:16:13,563
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if it's not your fault, as I often say,

216
00:16:15,584 --> 00:16:17,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially, you have to accept responsibility even for that.

217
00:16:18,848 --> 00:16:22,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your whole life up to that point was about avoidance of responsibility.

218
00:16:23,193 --> 00:16:29,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So now you have to accept responsibility for random circumstances and things that might happen to you that you generally didn't act out.

219
00:16:29,721 --> 00:16:35,808
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will have to accept responsibility until it sinks in, in your mind that, okay, no, this

220
00:16:37,468 --> 00:16:45,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Acceptance of responsibility should not be moving me, should not be pressuring me, should not be making me resist it and act out and trying to outrun it.

221
00:16:46,039 --> 00:16:54,790
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason it does so is because, well, because of the unwholesome habit of avoiding it through sensuality, acting out of ill will and distraction.

